  "notes": "The crossover pole of 90s country-pop — an adult-contemporary vocal record with a country band underneath, moving toward outright pop. Keys are the entry's most distinctive claim and are authored as stated: \"noticeably more flat and sharp keys than her male peers because the keys follow her vocal range rather than guitar shapes,\" so F♯/G/D/A♭/B carry the weight instead of the genre's open-guitar set. Drums encode the stated hybrid — \"loop-plus-live hybrid earlier than most of the genre — programmed shaker/tambourine 16ths over a live kit, kick on 1 and 'and-of-3,' chorus adds claps or a second snare layer\" — so the perc lanes run dense 16ths, the snare is layered, and the kick takes a 3& secondary anchor. Harmony adds the pop turnaround the entry names: ii–V–I plus a modal-borrowed ♭VI on bridges. The melody is described as \"the widest-range in this section — verses in a low, breathy register, choruses a full octave up with sustained belted notes,\" which is the two-octave register span and the raised octave-jump probability. ⚠ Confidence is medium (research: high-medium); the per-track tempo readings are [LOW-CONF] and the entry itself notes the uptempo 147–187 figures are doubled readings of a ~74–94 spine. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the perc density, the clap offset and the countermelody ratio — the entry gives \"programmed 16ths\" and \"claps or a second snare layer\" without numbers.",
